---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import ToolBench from '../components/tool/ToolBench.astro';
import FaqList from '../components/site/FaqList.astro';
import Disclaimer from '../components/site/Disclaimer.astro';
import ProCard from '../components/monetize/ProCard.astro';
import AdSlot from '../components/monetize/AdSlot.astro';
import { SITE } from '../data/site';
import { LINKS } from '../data/links';
import { FAQ_HOME } from '../data/faq';
import { orgNode, siteNode, webAppNode, webPageNode, faqNode } from '../lib/schema';

const title = 'Metadata Remover — Inspect and Remove EXIF, XMP, IPTC';
const description =
  'Remove EXIF, XMP, IPTC and JUMBF from your photos in the browser. Nothing is uploaded. Compare before and after, then download a cleaning report.';
const canonical = `${SITE.origin}/`;
const graph = [
  orgNode(),
  siteNode(),
  webAppNode(canonical, 'Metadata Remover', description),
  webPageNode(canonical, title),
  ...(FAQ_HOME.length > 0 ? [faqNode(canonical, FAQ_HOME)] : []),
];
---

<BaseLayout title={title} description={description} canonical={canonical} jsonLd={graph} h1="Metadata Remover">
  <h1>Metadata Remover</h1>
  <p class="lede">Photos in your browser. Nothing uploaded.</p>
  <p>
    A photo carries the device that took it, the time it was taken, and often the place. This tool reads
    those segments, removes them in your browser, and shows you exactly what changed.
  </p>

  <section class="tool-first" aria-label="Clean a file">
    <ToolBench
      variant="home"
      dropLabel="Drop a file here, or choose one"
      capture={false}
      accept="image/jpeg,image/png,image/webp,image/heic,image/heif"
      resultTitle="Your file"
    />
    <AdSlot pageId="home" slotId="home-after-result" />
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>What file metadata is, and what it carries</h2>
    <p>A photo holds more than pixels. It holds a record of how it was made.</p>
    <p>
      Alongside the image data, a file can store the camera or phone model, the lens, a serial number, the
      date and time, GPS coordinates, editing software, and a thumbnail. Some of that arrives from the
      camera, some is added later by an editor or an app. A metadata remover works on these extra segments
      rather than on the picture itself, so the pixels stay exactly as they were.
    </p>
    <p>
      People use a metadata remover for ordinary, practical reasons: a listing photo should not tell
      strangers where the item is stored, and a screenshot shared at work should not carry a device serial
      number. If you mainly handle phone pictures, the page about
      <a href={LINKS.photo.href}>photo metadata</a> goes field by field.
    </p>
  </section>

  <ProCard variant="result" pageId="home" />

  <section>
    <h2>Remove metadata in three steps</h2>
    <p>Pick a file, run the cleaner, read the report.</p>
    <p>
      First, drop a file onto the tool below or choose one from your device. Second, let the metadata
      remover strip the segments it knows about; the work happens on your device, and the file is not sent
      anywhere. Third, download the cleaned file and, if you want proof, open the report and compare the
      before and after lists. Removing metadata this way takes three clicks at most, and there is no
      account to create.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>Which formats work today</h2>
    <p>JPEG, PNG, WebP and HEIC work now. Video and PDF do not.</p>
    <p>
      For JPEG, the metadata remover deletes the EXIF, XMP, IPTC and JUMBF segments and keeps the color
      profile and image data. For PNG it removes text chunks and the EXIF chunk, including the prompt and
      workflow text some generators store. For WebP it removes the EXIF and XMP chunks and clears the
      matching header flags. HEIC files are cleaned at the container level. When you clean metadata here,
      the output is a new file; your original never changes.
    </p>
    <p>
      If you only need to remove metadata from a photo, the dedicated page explains the per-format
      details: <a href={LINKS.photo.href}>Remove metadata from a photo</a>.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="what-the-report-shows-you">
    <h2>What the report shows you</h2>
    <p>The report lists what was found, what was removed, and what was left alone.</p>
    <p>
      Every run of this metadata remover ends with a downloadable report in HTML and JSON. It records the
      conclusion, the scope of that conclusion, the output file's hash, and a segment-by-segment list with
      counts. Removed segments are confirmed by re-reading the cleaned file with the same parser, so the
      after-list is a fresh read, not a copy. The report never prints the original values of removed
      fields, because a report you share should not leak the very data you just removed.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>What in a file makes a platform label it as AI</h2>
    <p>Some platforms read a signature inside the file. Others read text an AI tool left behind.</p>
    <p>
      A file can carry Content Credentials in a manifest segment, an XMP field that declares a synthetic
      source, or plain text blocks with a prompt and generation settings. These are different things, and
      the report names them separately. Midjourney is a useful counterexample: as of early 2026 it does not
      write C2PA or IPTC metadata, so its images usually show nothing at all. Nothing found does not mean
      the file is not AI. Removing metadata changes what a file declares; it cannot change what a detector
      sees in the pixels, and it cannot change a label a platform already placed on a post.
    </p>
    {/* [3e] One-sentence link to the AI inner page goes here after W12; no H2 section allowed. */}
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>Common questions</h2>
    {/* FAQ_HOME: verbatim copy from phase3/3a_faq_copy.md, single source for visible list and JSON-LD. */}
    <FaqList faqs={FAQ_HOME} />
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>Pricing</h2>
    <p>The photo cleaner is free with no file limit.</p>
    <p>
      This metadata remover runs on your own device, so unlimited use costs us nothing to offer. Paid
      plans are about video, PDF and an ad-free page. <a href={LINKS.pricing.href}>See what is free and what is not</a>.
    </p>
  </section>

  <Disclaimer />
</BaseLayout>
